INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technical yarn producer compares two methods for producing a ''heather'' or ''melange'' yarn appearance (a mottled, multi-tone color effect from blending differently-colored fibers): blending DYED FIBER of two colors before spinning versus SPACE-DYEING a solid-color spun yarn afterward (discussed earlier). What VISUAL/textural difference distinguishes fiber-blended melange from space-dyed melange, even if both achieve a similarly ''mottled'' overall impression?', '["Fiber-blended melange and space-dyed melange always produce visually and texturally identical results, making any distinction between these two techniques purely theoretical with zero practical visual difference","Fiber-blended melange creates color variation at the MICROSCOPIC, individual-fiber level WITHIN every single section of yarn (each short length of yarn contains an intimate mix of both fiber colors), giving a fine, uniform, textile ''heathered'' grain throughout — space-dyed melange instead creates color variation at the MACRO level along the yarn''s length (each SEGMENT is a single solid color, with color changing between segments), giving a more segmented, sometimes streaky or blotchy visual pattern rather than the fine, even, grain-like texture of true fiber-blended heather","Space-dyed melange always produces a FINER, more microscopically uniform color blend than fiber-blended melange, the reverse of the actual visual/textural relationship between these two techniques","Fiber blending can only be used for solid, single-color yarn, making it technically incapable of producing any melange/mottled color effect, contradicting the basic premise of comparing it to space-dyeing"]'::jsonb, 'Fiber-blended melange creates color variation at the MICROSCOPIC, individual-fiber level WITHIN every single section of yarn (each short length of yarn contains an intimate mix of both fiber colors), giving a fine, uniform, textile ''heathered'' grain throughout — space-dyed melange instead creates color variation at the MACRO level along the yarn''s length (each SEGMENT is a single solid color, with color changing between segments), giving a more segmented, sometimes streaky or blotchy visual pattern rather than the fine, even, grain-like texture of true fiber-blended heather', 'This is a genuine, important visual/structural distinction between two DIFFERENT color-variation mechanisms — fiber-blended melange mixes differently-colored INDIVIDUAL FIBERS together before spinning, so every short section of the resulting yarn contains an intimate blend of both colors at the fiber level, creating the fine, even, classic ''heathered'' grain appearance; space-dyed yarn (discussed earlier) instead varies color along LONGER SEGMENTS of an already-spun yarn, meaning each segment is essentially solid-colored, with the color pattern changing between segments — while both achieve a broadly ''multi-color, mottled'' overall impression, the actual visual TEXTURE is genuinely different (fine, even heather grain vs. more segmented, sometimes streaky variation), an important distinction for a designer specifying which specific color-effect technique matches their intended aesthetic.', 3, '{"fiber-blend-vs-space-dye-melange"}'::text[]),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn technologist explains that recycled cotton (mechanically shredded/re-processed from pre- or post-consumer cotton textile waste, then re-spun) typically produces yarn with SIGNIFICANTLY shorter average fiber length than virgin cotton, directly limiting the achievable yarn count/quality. Why does the mechanical shredding/recycling process inherently shorten fiber length, connecting to earlier fiber-length discussions?', '["Mechanical shredding has no effect on fiber length whatsoever, making the described fiber-shortening phenomenon in recycled cotton technically unfounded","The mechanical process of shredding/tearing apart woven or knitted fabric structures to recover individual fibers inevitably BREAKS a significant proportion of the original fibers (since fibers are torn/pulled apart from their interlaced/interlooped structure rather than gently separated), producing an average fiber length considerably shorter than the original virgin fiber — this directly connects to the earlier short-fiber-content/spinning-limit discussion, since shorter average fiber length directly constrains achievable yarn fineness/quality (finer counts require longer fiber for adequate strength, as established earlier)","Recycled cotton always shows LONGER average fiber length than virgin cotton, the reverse of the actual, well-documented relationship between mechanical recycling and fiber length","Fiber length has no relationship to achievable yarn count or quality, making this entire discussion of recycled cotton''s fiber-length limitation technically irrelevant to yarn spinning capability"]'::jsonb, 'The mechanical process of shredding/tearing apart woven or knitted fabric structures to recover individual fibers inevitably BREAKS a significant proportion of the original fibers (since fibers are torn/pulled apart from their interlaced/interlooped structure rather than gently separated), producing an average fiber length considerably shorter than the original virgin fiber — this directly connects to the earlier short-fiber-content/spinning-limit discussion, since shorter average fiber length directly constrains achievable yarn fineness/quality (finer counts require longer fiber for adequate strength, as established earlier)', 'This connects the earlier spinning-limit/fiber-length and short-fiber-content discussions to a genuine sustainability-materials-science insight — mechanically recovering fiber from woven or knitted fabric requires physically tearing fibers apart from their interlaced/interlooped structure, which inevitably breaks/shortens a significant proportion of the original fiber length (unlike gentle separation, which isn''t practically achievable at the mechanical shredding scale needed for recycling) — since the earlier spinning-limit discussion established that finer, higher-quality yarn counts require adequate fiber length for sufficient inter-fiber cohesion/strength, this fiber-shortening effect is precisely why recycled cotton is typically limited to coarser counts or must be BLENDED with virgin fiber to achieve finer/higher-quality yarn specifications, a genuine, practically important technical constraint in sustainable/recycled fiber yarn production.', 3, '{"recycled-cotton-fiber-shortening"}'::text[]),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technical yarn lab compares chemically-recycled polyester (broken down to monomer and re-polymerized, as discussed earlier) against mechanically-recycled polyester on ACHIEVABLE draw ratio during filament production. Given the earlier discussion of mechanical recycling causing chain-scission/molecular-weight reduction, why would LOWER average molecular weight limit achievable draw ratio?', '["Molecular weight has no relationship to achievable draw ratio during filament production, making this entire technical comparison irrelevant to real yarn manufacturing","Achieving a high draw ratio requires the polymer chains to be long/entangled enough to develop strong, oriented crystalline structure under drawing WITHOUT the strand simply breaking prematurely — lower molecular weight (shorter average chain length, from mechanical recycling''s chain-scission damage) means fewer, weaker chain entanglements holding the strand together during the high-tension drawing process, limiting how much draw ratio can be applied before the filament breaks, which directly connects to why mechanically-recycled polyester often shows both lower achievable draw ratio AND correspondingly lower final tenacity than chemically-recycled or virgin polyester","Lower molecular weight always ALLOWS for higher achievable draw ratio, the reverse of the actual relationship between molecular weight and drawability established in polymer science","Draw ratio and molecular weight are measured using completely unrelated units with zero possible causal or correlational relationship between them, making this entire comparison technically unfounded"]'::jsonb, 'Achieving a high draw ratio requires the polymer chains to be long/entangled enough to develop strong, oriented crystalline structure under drawing WITHOUT the strand simply breaking prematurely — lower molecular weight (shorter average chain length, from mechanical recycling''s chain-scission damage) means fewer, weaker chain entanglements holding the strand together during the high-tension drawing process, limiting how much draw ratio can be applied before the filament breaks, which directly connects to why mechanically-recycled polyester often shows both lower achievable draw ratio AND correspondingly lower final tenacity than chemically-recycled or virgin polyester', 'This connects the earlier recycled-polyester/chain-scission discussion directly to the earlier draw-ratio/molecular-orientation/tenacity discussion — achieving high draw ratio requires sufficiently long, entangled polymer chains that can develop strong oriented crystalline structure under the high tension of drawing WITHOUT the strand simply snapping prematurely; mechanical recycling''s chain-scission damage (shorter average molecular weight) means fewer/weaker chain entanglements are available to hold the strand together during this high-stress drawing process, directly limiting the maximum achievable draw ratio before breakage — this is precisely why mechanically-recycled polyester filament often shows BOTH lower achievable draw ratio AND correspondingly lower final tenacity compared to chemically-recycled (molecular-weight-restored) or virgin polyester, connecting two separate earlier discussions (recycling degradation and draw-ratio/tenacity mechanics) into one unified technical explanation.', 3, '{"recycled-polyester-draw-ratio-limitation"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
